MITRE ATT@CK Journey

Tactic # 13 = Exfiltration


🎯 Goal
The adversary is trying to steal data from the victim's environment.

Think of it like this:

Collection = Gather the data. 📂

Exfiltration = Send the data outside the organization. 🌐

Without exfiltration, the attacker may have collected valuable information, but they haven't achieved their objective of taking it away.


What attackers exfiltrate:

Customer information
Financial records
Passwords and credential dumps
Databases
Source code
Intellectual Property (IP)
HR documents
Cloud data
Email archives


Common Exfiltration Methods:

HTTPS uploads
FTP / SFTP
DNS Tunneling
Cloud Storage (Google Drive, Dropbox, OneDrive, etc.)
Email attachments
USB devices
SMB shares
RDP clipboard or file transfer


Windows Event IDs & Telemetry

There is no dedicated Windows Event ID that says "Exfiltration."

Instead, SOC analysts correlate evidence from multiple sources.

4688 – Process Creation

Look for:

powershell.exe
curl.exe
certutil.exe
robocopy.exe
7z.exe


4663 – Object Access

Sensitive files accessed before transfer.


5145 – Network Share Access

Files accessed or copied through SMB shares.


5156 – Windows Filtering Platform

Allowed outbound network connections.

Also investigate:

Firewall logs
Proxy logs
DNS logs
EDR telemetry
SIEM correlations
DLP (Data Loss Prevention) alerts


🚩 Red Flags:

Large outbound data transfers
Uploads to unknown cloud services
Transfers outside business hours
.zip, .rar, or .7z archives leaving the network
Unusual outbound HTTPS traffic
Sensitive files copied to removable media


SOC Investigation Checklist

Whenever you detect possible exfiltration, ask:

✅ Who transferred the data?
✅ What data was transferred?
✅ Where was it sent?
✅ When did it happen?
✅ Why was it transferred?
✅ How much data left the network?
✅ Was the data compressed first?
✅ Was the destination trusted?
✅ Was the transfer successful?
✅ Has this user done similar activity before?



Real-World Example

An attacker:

Accesses confidential files (4663).
Compresses them with 7z.exe (4688).
Uses PowerShell to upload the archive to an external cloud service.
Firewall or proxy logs show a large outbound HTTPS upload.
The SOC team correlates these events and confirms Exfiltration.


🧠 SOC Analyst Tip

Don't investigate only the upload.

Build the entire timeline:



4663 → Sensitive files accessed
        ↓
4688 → 7z.exe creates archive
        ↓
4688 → powershell.exe starts
        ↓
5156 → Outbound HTTPS connection
        ↓
Firewall/Proxy → Large upload detected
        ↓
Possible Data Exfiltration


This timeline tells the full story of the attack.


